# Copy to deployment/.env and fill both in.
#
# The name matters. Compose reads this file for two different jobs —
# substituting ${AUTHENTIK_PG_PASS} into the compose file, and passing
# variables into the containers — and only a file called .env does both. An
# env_file: entry covers the second job alone, so a differently named file
# fails interpolation before anything starts.
#
# Neither value has a default on purpose: an identity provider with a shipped
# secret is worse than one that refuses to start.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 36   # once for each
#
AUTHENTIK_SECRET_KEY=
AUTHENTIK_PG_PASS=

# Uncomment to stop authentik sending crash reports to its authors.
# AUTHENTIK_ERROR_REPORTING__ENABLED=false

# Dex — the light identity provider, for docker-compose.dex.yml. One container
# instead of authentik's three, and every setting in dex/config.yaml rather
# than in a database, so a fresh clone gets the same provider.
#
# The two are alternatives. Run one: a stack with two issuers is a stack where
# the brain trusts one of them and rejects the other's tokens.
#
#   make dex-hash    # reads the password on stdin, prints the line below
#
# Single quotes are not optional. A bcrypt hash contains $, and compose reads
# an unquoted one as a variable reference — $2y$10$abc becomes y0abc.
DEX_PASSWORD_HASH=
# DEX_PORT=5556
#
# The `sub` claim for the dev user in dex/config.yaml. Committed rather than
# read back after every reset, because dex derives it from the userID pinned
# in that file — deleting the dex volume and logging in again produces this
# same string. Verified by doing exactly that.
#
# OPENARITY_OIDC_ENABLED=true
# OPENARITY_OIDC_ISSUER=http://<your LAN address>:5556
# OPENARITY_OIDC_AUDIENCE=openarity
# OPENARITY_SUPER_ADMINS=CiQwZDFlOWYzYy02YTUyLTRmNWQtOGI3MS0yYzRlNmE4ZDBmMTMSBWxvY2Fs
#
# No trailing slash on the issuer, unlike authentik's. Copy whatever
# `jq .issuer` on the discovery document returns.

# The brain's OpenBao credentials, for the docker-compose.openbao.yml overlay.
# Not needed for the default stack, which runs OpenBao in dev mode.
#
# These are minted, not chosen — `make bao-approle` prints both lines ready to
# paste. A secret_id is a credential with a lease: reissue it with the same
# command rather than sharing one between deployments.
#
# See deployment/OPENBAO.md.
OPENARITY_SECRETS_APPROLE_ID=
OPENARITY_SECRETS_APPROLE_SECRET=

# The object store, for the docker-compose.objects.yml overlay. Not needed for
# the default stack, which starts no object store at all.
#
# SeaweedFS's admin credential and the brain's are the same pair here, which is a
# development convenience rather than a pattern: `make objects` seeds the
# server with these and prints the four exports the live tests read. In a real
# deployment the brain gets a credential scoped to one bucket and the root user
# is not it.
#
# OBJECTS_BUCKET=openarity
# OBJECTS_ACCESS_KEY=openarity
# OBJECTS_SECRET_KEY=openarity-dev-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Staging
#
# `make staging` sets nothing by itself — it starts authentik, the real
# OpenBao, SeaweedFS and the published image, and the brain reads its settings
# from here. These five lines are what turn the default development stack into
# one that survives a restart.
#
# The two backends default to static and memory, which hold everything in the
# process. Both are refused once ENVIRONMENT is not development, so a staging
# brain that is missing these does not start quietly with nowhere to put
# anything — it exits naming the setting.
#
# OPENARITY_ENVIRONMENT=staging
# OPENARITY_SECRETS_BACKEND=openbao
# OPENARITY_OBJECTS_BACKEND=seaweedfs
#
# The endpoint defaults to http://seaweedfs:8333, which is where the overlay's
# SeaweedFS listens inside the compose network. The host port it is published
# on is for you and the tests, not for the brain. Set it only when the store is
# somewhere else, and use s3 as the backend for anything that is not SeaweedFS.
#
# OPENARITY_OBJECTS_ENDPOINT=http://seaweedfs:8333
#
# The three OBJECTS_ lines above configure both SeaweedFS and the brain, so
# uncomment them too — a staging brain with no credential reaches the store
# and is refused.
#
# ENVIRONMENT=staging also refuses DEV_TOKEN outright, so OIDC stops being
# optional. Set OPENARITY_OIDC_ENABLED=true and the issuer with it, and drop
# "dev" from SUPER_ADMINS — see the authentik section of README.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTPS on names you own, for docker-compose.https.yml. start-docker.sh adds
# that file when HTTPS_HOST is set, and only with authentik.
#
# Needed whenever the dashboard is opened at anything but 127.0.0.1: a browser
# signs in only from a secure context, and a LAN or tailnet address over plain
# HTTP is not one, so the dashboard there says it cannot sign you in.
#
# Both names get a DNS-only (grey cloud) A record pointing at BIND_ADDR. A
# private address works: the certificate is proven through Cloudflare's API,
# so nothing on the internet has to reach this machine. The token needs
# Zone -> DNS -> Edit on that one zone and nothing else.
#
# HTTPS_HOST=dashboard.example.com
# HTTPS_AUTH_HOST=auth.example.com
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The webhook listener on the internet, for docker-compose.tunnel.yml, which
# start-docker.sh adds when the token is set. Only the listener: the dashboard
# and API stay where BIND_ADDR puts them.
#
# Create a tunnel in Cloudflare (Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels), copy its
# token, and give it one public hostname: HOOKS_HOST -> HTTP brain:21121.
# One level under the zone, since the tunnel's name is proxied and the free
# certificate covers one level.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=
# HOOKS_HOST=hooks.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A model gateway, for docker-compose.litellm.yml or docker-compose.omniroute.yml.
# Not needed for the default stack, which starts neither.
#
# The two are alternatives answering the same endpoint, so fill in one block.
# Both are heavy — 1.2 GB and 4.14 GB — and neither belongs on a small machine
# alongside Postgres. See the header of either compose file for the numbers.

# LiteLLM. Three values, none with a default: the master key is the credential
# that mints every other key, and a gateway with a shipped password is worse
# than one that refuses to start.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 36   # once for each
#
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
LITELLM_PG_PASS=
LITELLM_UI_PASSWORD=
# LITELLM_UI_USERNAME=admin
# LITELLM_PORT=14000

# OmniRoute. The image defaults this to CHANGEME and logs a warning nobody
# reads, so the compose file requires it instead.
OMNIROUTE_PASSWORD=
# OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=
# OMNIROUTE_PORT=20128

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The graph: agents, tools and skills, findable by meaning. Off until a model
# is named, and the worker says so when it starts without one.
#
# Where to ask, what to ask for, and how wide the answer is. The base URL is
# anything that answers OpenAI's /embeddings; the model is the name that
# endpoint knows it by; the dimensions are the width of the vectors it
# returns. All three are set together or not at all. The index is built for
# one width, so changing the model means `brain graph rebuild`.
#
# `make ollama` runs a model on this machine that needs no account and no key:
#
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
#
# A provider, or a gateway in front of one, wants a key. It is the key itself,
# sent as a bearer token and never logged:
#
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# OPENARITY_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1536
#
# The chat models are a separate endpoint with a separate key, because they
# are often a separate service. The worker calls it to run agents.
#
# OPENARITY_LLM_BASE_URL=http://omniroute:20128/v1
# OPENARITY_LLM_API_KEY=
#
# OLLAMA_PORT=11434

# Provider credentials, read by whichever gateway is running. LiteLLM proxies
# every model with a wildcard and routes on the name's prefix, so a key here is
# all a provider needs — anthropic/claude-opus-5 and openai/gpt-5 work with no
# further configuration.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
